# The Global Interpreter Lock: CPU Contention vs I/O Release
direction: right
classes: {
  mode_card: { style: { fill: "#111827"; stroke-width: 2; border-radius: 10 } }
}

cpu: "CPU-Bound: Thread Contention (Negative Speedup)\n• Only 1 Python thread holds GIL mutex at any instant\n• Remaining threads forced to wait on sys.getswitchinterval() (5ms)\n• OS context-switching thrashing degrades performance\n• Multi-threading is SLOWER than single-thread for CPU tasks!\n✓ Production Fix: Use Multiprocessing, Cython, or Polars" {
  class: mode_card
  style.stroke: "#EF4444"
}

io: "I/O-Bound: Concurrency Acceleration (GIL Released)\n• CPython runtime releases GIL before blocking OS syscalls\n• Threads execute in parallel during disk, database & socket I/O\n• Operating system handles asynchronous kernel events\n• Near-linear scalability for network scrapers & microservices\n✓ Production Fix: Use ThreadPoolExecutor or Asyncio" {
  class: mode_card
  style.stroke: "#10B981"
}

cpu -> io: "VS" {
  style.stroke: "#F59E0B"
}